":"ort E"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"PS.\n- It"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" ignores debt,"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" c"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ash flow and growth r"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ate. The"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" PEG ratio (P"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"/E divided by ear"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"nings growth) and"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" E"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"V/EBITDA help"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" f"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ill those gaps."},{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n- Typ"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ical P/Es vary a"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" lot by sector."},{"type":"text_delta","text":" Tech us"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ually trades higher than ut"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ilities or banks."},{"type":"text_delta","text":"\n\nUse it as a qu"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ick scre"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ening tool al"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"ongside other met"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"rics,"},{"type":"text_delta","text":" not as a stand"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"alone bu"},{"type":"text_delta","text":"y or sell signal."},{"type":"completed","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The P/E (price-to-earnings) ratio compares a company's share price to its earnings per share (EPS):\n\n**P/E = share price ÷ earnings per share**\n\n**What it tells you**\n- **How much investors pay per dollar of earnings.** A P/E of 20 means investors pay $20 for every $1 of the company's annual profit.\n- **Market expectations.** A high P/E often means investors expect strong growth. A low P/E can mean low expectations, a mature business, or a stock that is undervalued.\n- **Relative valuation.** It is most useful for comparing similar companies, such as two banks, or a company against its own history or its industry average.\n\n**Common variants**\n- **Trailing P/E** uses the last 12 months of actual earnings.\n- **Forward P/E** uses analysts' earnings estimates for the next 12 months.\n\n**Caveats**\n- A **high P/E** isn't automatically \"expensive\", and a **low P/E** isn't automatically a \"bargain\". A low P/E can be a value trap if earnings are about to fall.\n- It's **not meaningful for companies with negative or very volatile earnings**.\n- Accounting choices and one-off items can distort EPS.\n- It ignores debt, cash flow and growth rate. The PEG ratio (P/E divided by earnings growth) and EV/EBITDA help fill those gaps.\n- Typical P/Es vary a lot by sector. Tech usually trades higher than utilities or banks.\n\nUse it as a quick screening tool alongside other metrics, not as a standalone buy or sell signal."}]},"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":513,"cache_read_input_tokens":4517,"cache_creation_input_tokens":90},"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","refusal_category":null}]}
